- About 100 billion galaxies.
- Holy. Seriously?

00:10:07.960 --> 00:10:08.880
- Yeah.

00:10:08.880 --> 00:10:10.600
About 100 billion.

00:10:10.600 --> 00:10:12.920
- Dang, weird.
- Oh man.

00:10:12.920 --> 00:10:14.160
(participants laugh)
- It's huge.

00:10:14.160 --> 00:10:16.040
100 billion are observable.

00:10:16.040 --> 00:10:18.280
- And these are just galaxies?
- These are galaxies.

00:10:18.280 --> 00:10:21.600
Each galaxy then has
about 100 billion stars.

00:10:21.600 --> 00:10:22.720
- Okay.
- Right?

00:10:22.720 --> 00:10:24.120
That's mind blowing a little bit.

00:10:24.120 --> 00:10:25.000
- Right?
- Yeah.

00:10:25.000 --> 00:10:25.800
- This is what I'm saying.

00:10:25.800 --> 00:10:26.960
I feel like most people don't know

00:10:26.960 --> 00:10:28.440
the scale of the universe,

00:10:28.440 --> 00:10:29.800
and that's what I'm
out here to talk about.

00:10:29.800 --> 00:10:30.640
- Mm-hmm.
- That's why I wanted to be,

00:10:30.640 --> 00:10:34.200
like, it's so much bigger
than almost anyone thinks.

00:10:34.200 --> 00:10:35.040
- Right.

00:10:35.040 --> 00:10:36.640
- In fact, there could be even more,

00:10:36.640 --> 00:10:39.880
